ows. That is, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ij"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" will match the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th symbol in the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th row. Now we get the usual system of linear equations. Note that we have a restriction on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/950c4b2aba198534045e9873d46c08a6bf5d311726aa69a87ddc8a552752d452.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", that is, we are interested in the number of SLE solutions, modulo 5. Then for each query it would be possible to find the number of solutions using the Gauss algorithm (0 or 5"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"rk"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), but this solution will take "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time, which does not fit under restrictions. One of the optimizations for improving the algorithm is to bring the matrix "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to a triangular matrix form (find the rank) and memorize the transformations. Then for each query string we could apply these transformations, then the algorithm will work "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), which is already past Time Limit."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"kind":"text","text":" Solution "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Loud_Scream"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Loud_Scream"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/832/submission/28853611"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"28853611"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/53461"}